From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Justin Walsh <contact.me@thejustinwalsh.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 13:24:37 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] moe cache: LLAMA_MOE_CACHE_QUEUED_REFILL, refills on a second
 CUDA stream, published when their event has completed

Strata code in this patch: Copyright (c) 2026 Niko1221 and the Strata contributors, MIT License
(LICENSE added in Strata c0107c3; the full notice is engines/patches/llama-upstream-flash/LICENSE.strata).
Ported from src/program/generate.cpp @ d551edf :3302-3370 (`adapt` / `apply_pending`: a swap marks its victim
uncached first, copies the newcomer on the adapt stream, and publishes the new residency only once the copy's
event has completed) and src/core/expert_cache.cpp @ d551edf :268 and :285 (`fill_slot_queued`,
`sync_queued`). NOT ported: Strata's issuer thread and its pageable-blob stager (src/prefill/prefill.cpp @
d551edf :122-239, :945-1003), which hide the cost of copying unpinned blobs; our experts are pinned (0005), so
cudaMemcpyAsync returns at once and there is nothing for a thread to hide. With pageable experts the new path
is not used.

Off unless LLAMA_MOE_CACHE_QUEUED_REFILL=1; off, the code path is the one 0003/0004 have (the upload thread and
the per-entry table writes). It also falls back, with a warning, when the cached layers are not all on one
device, when the experts are not in pinned host memory (LLAMA_PIN_EXPERTS=1), or when the device cannot query an
event without blocking.

What the cache did, read from src/llama-moecache.cpp: the copies were already off the decode thread (a worker
thread, ggml_backend_tensor_set per matrix: a copy and a stream synchronise each) and published only at a later
step(); what ran on the decode thread, every adaptive step, was the table writes -- one synchronous device copy
per evicted expert and another per newcomer, up to 2 x 96. (docs/FLASH-NEXT.md 10.3 said the copies ran on the
compute stream in step(); they did not.)

Now (src/llama-moecache-refill.{h,cpp}, src/llama-moecache.cpp):
- no worker thread. A step's evictions are written to the layers' host mirrors, then each changed layer's device and
  host table is written ONCE, whole (so no graph can see an evicted slot), then the newcomers' copies (the three
  matrices of each expert) are enqueued on a second backend instance's stream (backend_engine) and closed into a
  batch with an event;
- the next steps poll the batches' events WITHOUT blocking (cudaEventQuery), and only the jobs of a batch whose event
  has completed are published: slot_expert / expert_slot updated and the table written (queue::poll, publish,
  flush_tables). A batch that has not landed stays; publication is in order. The adaptive tier still starts no
  swap while one is in flight; the LRU policy does not queue an expert whose copy is already in flight
  (queue::contains) -- a copy now lands a few steps later than a thread's did;
- ggml-cuda offers the non-blocking query as the registry's proc address "ggml_backend_event_query"
  (ggml-cuda.cu), like ggml's other optional backend features, so no public ggml header changes (a change to
  ggml-backend.h or -impl.h rebuilds every CUDA object);
- LLAMA_MOE_REFILL_POISON=1 (tests; both paths): a slot is filled with 0xFF before its refill is enqueued, so a
  slot read before its copy landed shows up as NaN; the hit-rate line gains "refills: batches, experts,
  in-flight, steps to land avg/max".

Tests (tests/test-moe-refill.cpp, registered with ctest): the harness runs the same functions step() calls (queue
push/poll, enqueue_job with the poison, publish, flush_tables) over a layer bookkeeping with the cache's member
names, with random swaps, and after every operation checks that every expert the device table points at sits in a
slot holding exactly that expert's bytes (all three matrices) -- a slot is never published before its copy has
landed. With a fake engine whose copies land only when the test lands them, at random: 400 steps, 3 layers, 1,221
refills, 399 of 400 steps found a batch still in flight, steps to land avg 3.58 max 13: 846 checks, 0 failed; a
harness that publishes at push time (the control) is caught in 40 of 40 steps; the queue alone publishes only the
landed prefix, oldest first. With --device NAME (the caller restricts CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES): the real engine, eight
64 MiB copies queued ahead of every batch so that it lands late, the same invariant, polls must find batches not yet
landed, and the event query must say "not yet" behind 512 MiB of copies and "done" after. NOT run on a GPU yet and
nothing is measured: whether it speeds the decode is the gate's question (docs/FLASH-NEXT.md 10.3).
